sources:

# Local sources only
cass runs fully local-only. `cass sources add` indexes extra local
directories under a name; there is no remote discovery, sync, or
scheduling. Old `type = "ssh"` entries in sources.toml are inert
archive records: their already-mirrored conversations stay searchable
and source-filterable, and ordinary `cass index` keeps indexing any
existing <data-dir>/remotes/<name>/mirror directories read-only.

  cass sources add archive-box --path /Volumes/old-sessions
  cass sources add archive-box -p /data/a -p /data/b   # repeatable
  cass sources list                      # human summary
  cass sources list --json               # kind: local | legacy-remote
  cass sources remove archive-box        # drop the config entry
  cass sources remove archive-box --purge  # + delete its mirror data
  cass sources doctor --json             # local checks only

Removed remote verbs (each fails with a removed-command error):
  sources sync, sources setup, sources discover, sources reingest,
  sources artifact-manifest, sources schedule, `sources add user@host`

# Workspace path mappings
Path mappings rewrite archived remote workspace paths to local
equivalents so `cass view`/`cass tui` open hits at the right place.

  cass sources mappings list
  cass sources mappings add laptop --from /home/u/proj --to /work/proj
  cass sources mappings test laptop /home/u/proj/x
  cass sources mappings remove laptop 0   # index from list output

# Persisted agent indexing exclusions
Use this when you want cass to ignore a connector entirely even if
the backing session files still exist on disk.

  cass sources agents list
  cass sources agents list --json
  cass sources agents exclude openclaw
  cass sources agents exclude openclaw --keep-indexed-data
  cass sources agents include openclaw

Configuration is persisted in ~/.config/cass/sources.toml as:
  disabled_agents = ["openclaw"]
By default, `exclude` also purges already archived local data for that agent
and rebuilds lexical search from the remaining archive. Use
`--keep-indexed-data` if you only want to block future indexing.
Exclusions apply to future local scans, archive mirror scans, and watch mode.
